{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00860 - MONDO_11508",
  "verbalization": "Prednisolone is implicated in the pathogenesis of Lymphoma, Non-hodgkin, Familial through a mechanism involving the use of Prednisone, a corticosteroid, as an indirect therapeutic agent. The evidence suggests that Prednisolone, through its indication for Autoimmune Hemolytic Anemia, Chronic Primary Adrenal Insufficiency, and Chondrocalcinosis, is linked to the development of Lymphoma, Non-hodgkin, Familial. These conditions, in turn, are associated with a systemic inflammatory or metabolic disorder that may contribute to the progression of lymphoproliferative diseases. The shared pathway from Prednisolone to Prednisone, and then to the target condition, highlights a potential role of corticosteroids in modulating immune responses and inflammatory processes, which could be relevant in the pathophysiology of non-hodgkin lymphoma. The evidence underscores a possible link between corticosteroid use and the development of this lymphoma subtype, though the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated.",
